      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q4-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) Start with 4e2x, translate down 28 and reflect the part below the horizontal axis upwards: The cusp satisfies 4e2x = 28, hence x = ln 7/2. The graph approaches y = 28 [B1] [A1] as x →−∞, falls through (0, 24) to (1 2 ln 7, 0), and then rises: [A1] (b) For x &lt; 0, the root satisfies G(x) = 4e2x + 3x + 12 . [B1] [A1] = 0 The branch equation is 28 −4e2x = 3x + 40. Substitution at the rounding boundaries gives approximately −0.000024 and 0.000006. Since G(−4.000455) &lt; 0 &lt; G(−4.000445) and G is continuous and strictly increasing, α = −4.00045 [M1] [A1] to five decimal places: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q4-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"2 ln 7, f(x) ≤28, so the equation has no root there: [M1] For h(x) = 4e2x −3x −68, (c) For 0 ≤x ≤1 h(1) ≈−41.444 &lt; 0 and h(2) ≈144.393 &gt; 0; continuity places the positive root between 1 and 2. On the upper exponential branch rearrange 4e2x −3x −68 = 0 to xn+1 = 1 2 ln[(3xn + 68)/4]. [M1] To derive the iteration write 4e2x = 3x + 68, e2x = (3x + 68)/4, 2x = ln[(3x + 68)/4]. With x1 = 1.5, the next values are 1.448646, 1.447582, 1.447560, . . .; the later iterates approach 1.447559955 . . .. Substitution gives x4 = 1.447560422 . . . and x5 = 1.447559965 . . ., correcting the last digits in the scan: The derivative of the iteration function is at most 3/142 &lt; 1 [A1] on the invariant interval [1, 2]. [A1] Thus β = 1.44756 [A1] to five decimal places: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 4 is 14 marks.</p>"
