    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"A curve has equation   π    π  y = ln tan  x +  , where tan  x +  &gt; 0 .     4    4  Show that dy dx = 2sec2 x . ( ) 6\" style=\"--madas-width:588.0px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Write u = x + π/4. Differentiating ln(tan u) by the chain rule gives dy/dx = sec2 u/ tan u. [M1] Writing both factors in sines and cosines gives (cos u/ sin u)/ cos2 u = 1/(sin u cos u). [M1] Use the compound-angle formulae: √ √ 2 2 sin u = 2 (sin x + cos x), cos u = 2 (cos x −sin x). [M1] [A1] Use (sin x + cos x)(cos x −sin x) = sin x cos x −sin2 x + cos2 x −cos x sin x. Multiplying gives sin u cos u = (cos2 x −sin2 x)/2. [A1] Hence dy/dx = 2/(cos2 x −sin2 x)\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"= 2/ cos(2x) . [A1] = 2 sec(2x)\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 6 marks.</p>"
    ],
