      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q10-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"The parametric route gives dy/dx = (−2 sin 2θ)/(4 cos θ) = −sin θ where cos θ̸ = 0, with the endpoint slope obtained by a limit. Write the tangent at (4 sin θ, cos 2θ) and substitute (3, 0): y −cos 2θ = −sin θ(x −4 sin θ) 0 −cos 2θ = −sin θ(3 −4 sin θ) −cos 2θ = −3 sin θ + 4 sin2 θ −(1 −2 sin2 θ) = −3 sin θ + 4 sin2 θ −1 + 2 sin2 θ = −3 sin θ + 4 sin2 θ 0 = 2 sin2 θ −3 sin θ + 1 (2 sin θ −1)(sin θ −1) = 0 The values sin θ = 1/2, 1 give (2, 1 −2(1/2)2) and (4, 1 −2). Alternatively, eliminate θ using cos 2θ = 1 −2 sin2 θ. [M1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-pure-v/q10-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"Then y = 1 −x2/8 [A1] for 0 ≤x ≤4. [A1] At x = p, the tangent has slope −p/4, including the one-sided tangent at p = 4. [A1] Substituting (3, 0) into its equation gives 0 = 1 −p2 8 −p 4(3 −p) ⇐⇒ (p −2)(p −4) = 0. [M1] [A1] Thus P = (2, 1/2) or (4, −1) . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
